r  everything when it comes to that stuff  yeah yeah  um so this is the longest bond movie uh  to date and i'm curious did you have a  like a longer cut that you like you know  what i mean like how long was your  director's cut if you will of the film  it wasn't that much longer uh i had an  assembly of the film 10 days after  wrapping that was just under three hours  so over the course of the next like 12  weeks we trimmed it down another 17  minutes tighten things maybe swapped out  some takes but that was it  oh wow um did you end up with like any  deleted scenes or was it more like  taking the air out of the room in  certain sequences  there was  mainly just taking the out of the room  and tightening you know dropping lines  dialogue uh there was a couple sort of  action sequences we might have truncated  just a little bit  but um and some connective tissue as  well you know you know getting from  point a to point b and that's where most  of tightening took place  do you believe that fans will ever see  like the the stuff you cut out or do you  do you believe in the whole once it's  out of the movie it's never going to be  seen  it's funny you know if you think about  the early 2000s and all those kind of  deleted scenes extras that you saw on  dvds you know i'd watch them and always  be like oh i'm glad they didn't put that  in  you know  so i likewise i think if they ever did  dvd extras i would i would say  personally i think if a scene was  deleted there's probably a reason it was  deleted  sure unless we're talking about the  kingdom of heaven director's cut in  which case it's a masterpiece and people  need to see it just bringing up ridley  scott oh really  yeah no oh my god someone told me  there's like a four-hour version  or a five-hour version of magnolia and  i i actually would really love to see  the five hour version of magnolia  i would pay someone big money to see  that version of the movie i would  i would but i'm telling you if you  haven't seen the extended cut of kingdom  of heaven it's like my favorite film of  that year it's 40 something minutes of  extra footage and it completely changes  the film that that's i'll look for it  um i strongly recommend it so um i think  this is the issue with contemporary  filmmaking is that you know studio is  driven by sort of i think i can't say  why they think all the time but the kind  of the runtime issue that goes for  television too  driven by you know  the kind of mediocre or the median line  of content you know that keeps people's  eyes engaged in projects drives even  uh the length the running time of films  that shouldn't be driven by that by that  tenant and it's like you know david lean  made incredible films that were four or  five hours long and i just wish i wish a  story can just be the length it needs to  be and not try to fit into the box just  so you know just just in case you know  people are afraid they won't pay  attention  yeah listen i yeah we could have a whole  conversation on this and i believe me i  i  uh yes i fully agree with what you're  saying um so i love the warner in the  movie and i'm just curious you've done  obviously some really cool wonders in  the past um did you have a lot of talk  about did you try to do even more in  this movie or is even just doing do you  know what i mean like what because it's  challenging to film these and with the  time and the budget and everything could  you i guess could you talk about it  yeah i've i've avoided talking about it  when people have asked me if there's if  we can expect something like that in  this film because i'd rather you know  people watch the film without any  expectation of a kind of craft piece  somewhere in the middle because i always  feel like that disengages the suspense  of the suspension of disbelief that  you're just striving for the entire time  but it in terms of  execution and trying to find ways to do  this you know  it's really interesting like even in  matera there